### Configure nested routes with createBrowserRouter and Outlet

Source: https://reactrouter.com/start/data/routing

Defines parent and child route hierarchies where child paths automatically inherit the parent path, rendering matching children inside the parent component's Outlet.

```typescript
createBrowserRouter([
  {
    path: "/dashboard",
    Component: Dashboard,
    children: [
      { index: true, Component: Home },
      { path: "settings", Component: Settings },
    ],
  },
]);
```

```tsx
import { Outlet } from "react-router";

export default function Dashboard() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      {/* will either be <Home> or <Settings> */}
      <Outlet />
    </div>
  );
}
```

--------------------------------

### Configuring Data Mode with createBrowserRouter and RouterProvider

Source: https://reactrouter.com/start/modes

Move route configuration outside React rendering to enable data loaders, actions, pending states, and fetchers.

```tsx
import {
  createBrowserRouter,
  RouterProvider,
} from "react-router";

let router = createBrowserRouter([
  {
    path: "/",
    Component: Root,
    loader: loadRootData,
  },
]);

ReactDOM.createRoot(root).render(
  <RouterProvider router={router} />,
);
```

--------------------------------

### Create router with createBrowserRouter and render with RouterProvider

Source: https://reactrouter.com/start/data/installation

Creates a browser router outside the React component tree and renders it using RouterProvider. Data Routers should not be held in React state.

```tsx
import React from "react";
import ReactDOM from "react-dom/client";
import { createBrowserRouter } from "react-router";
import { RouterProvider } from "react-router/dom";

const router = createBrowserRouter([
  {
    path: "/",
    element: <div>Hello World</div>,
  },
]);

const root = document.getElementById("root");

ReactDOM.createRoot(root).render(
  <RouterProvider router={router} />,
);
```

### Layout Routes

Source: https://reactrouter.com/start/data/routing

Omitting the `path` in a route creates new [Nested Routes](#nested-routes) for its children without adding any segments to the URL.

Note that:

- `Home` and `Contact` will be rendered into the `MarketingLayout` outlet
- `Project` and `EditProject` will be rendered into the `ProjectLayout` outlet while `ProjectsHome` will not.

--------------------------------

### createMemoryRouter > Summary

Source: https://reactrouter.com/api/data-routers/createMemoryRouter

[Reference Documentation ↗](https://api.reactrouter.com/v8/functions/react-router.createMemoryRouter.html)

Create a new [`DataRouter`](https://api.reactrouter.com/v8/interfaces/react-router.DataRouter.html) that manages the application path using an
in-memory [`History`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/History)
stack. Useful for non-browser environments without a DOM API.

Data Routers should not be held in React state. You should create your router
once outside of the React tree and pass it to [`<RouterProvider>`](../data-routers/RouterProvider).
You can use `patchRoutesOnNavigation` to add additional routes programmatically.

